  "short": "Public aid that guarantees a minimum standard of living and supports independence",
  "target": "Households whose income, even after making full use of their assets, abilities, and everything else available to them, falls below the minimum living cost calculated under the standards set by the Minister of Health, Labour and Welfare. An application can be made even by a person who has no housing or whose documents are not complete. A household that owns a home or a car used for commuting may, depending on how these are used, still qualify while keeping them.",
  "benefit": "Each month, the difference between the minimum living cost and income such as pensions and earnings from work is paid as assistance. There are 8 types of aid: livelihood aid (food, clothing, utilities, etc.), housing aid (actual rent and similar costs paid within set limits), education aid, medical aid (no out-of-pocket cost), long-term care aid (no out-of-pocket cost), childbirth aid, occupational aid (costs of acquiring skills, upper secondary school attendance costs, etc.), and funeral aid. Standard amounts vary by region and household composition. From October of Reiwa 7 (2025), as a temporary and exceptional measure for fiscal years Reiwa 7 and 8 (FY2025-FY2026, through March 31 of Reiwa 9 (2027)), a special addition of 1,500 yen per person per month is applied to livelihood aid (a monthly 1,000 yen is maintained for hospital inpatients and residents of long-term care facilities). The review of the livelihood aid standards for FY2026 indicates that, from October of Reiwa 8 (2026), this special addition is to be raised by a further 1,000 yen, to 2,500 yen per person per month, for a period of 1 year (a monthly 1,000 yen is maintained for hospital inpatients and residents of long-term care facilities).",
