 "yoikuhi-minpo-kaisei": {
  "name": "Revisions to the Civil Code and Related Acts on Child Support (in force 2026-04-01, Reiwa 8)",
  "short": "New child support rules, including statutory child support and a statutory lien",
  "target": "Single parents caring for a child after divorce or similar circumstances, parents who are about to divorce, and people involved in child support agreements and payments. Whether the rules apply depends on the timing of the divorce or of the agreement.",
  "benefit": "The Act Partially Amending the Civil Code and Other Acts (Act No. 33 of 2024, Reiwa 6) came into force on April 1, 2026 (2026-04-01, Reiwa 8). (1) Statutory child support: even where a couple divorced without making a child support agreement, a new mechanism allows a provisional and supplementary claim of 20,000 yen per month for 1 child (set by Ministry of Justice ordinance) from the time of the divorce. (2) Statutory lien: a lien is attached to child support claims, allowing payment to be received ahead of other creditors for the portion up to 80,000 yen per month for 1 child. Alongside this, the rules on parental authority after the parents' divorce (including the option of joint parental authority) and on parent-child contact were also revised.",
  "window": "Houterasu, the Japan Legal Support Center (法テラス(日本司法支援センター)); the family court (家庭裁判所); and the municipal single-parent consultation counter (市区町村のひとり親相談窓口) with its self-reliance support workers for single mothers and fathers (母子・父子自立支援員)",
  "documents": [
   "(When making an agreement) a divorce agreement or notarized deed, or a family court mediation record or adjudication document",
   "(When consulting) materials showing income and the child's situation are said to make consultation easier"
  ],
  "steps": [
   "First consult the Houterasu support dial (weekdays 9:00 to 21:00, Saturdays 9:00 to 17:00) or the municipal single-parent consultation counter",
   "Where there is no child support agreement, aim to reach one through discussion between the parties or through family court mediation or adjudication",
   "While an agreement remains difficult, consider claiming statutory child support (20,000 yen per month for 1 child)",
   "If payments fall behind, consult a specialist counter about options such as the statutory lien and compulsory execution"
  ],
  "notes": [
   "Statutory child support does not apply where the divorce took place before the date of entry into force (2026-04-01, Reiwa 8)",
   "The statutory lien applies to child support agreed before the date of entry into force as well, for the portions falling due on or after that date",
   "Statutory child support is described as a provisional and supplementary mechanism that lasts until an agreement is made, and not as a substitute for a proper agreement reached through discussion or mediation based on the calculation tables and similar references",
   "Whether it applies to an individual case needs to be confirmed through legal consultation (Houterasu, a lawyer, and so on)"
  ],
  "recent": "The act amending the Civil Code and other acts, enacted in May 2024 (Reiwa 6), came into force on April 1, 2026 (2026-04-01, Reiwa 8). The Ministry of Justice ordinance setting the statutory child support amount (20,000 yen per month for 1 child) and the amount covered by the statutory lien (80,000 yen per month for 1 child), Ministry of Justice Ordinance No. 56 of 2025 (Reiwa 7), was established on December 12, 2025 (2025-12-12, Reiwa 7)."
 },
